We would like to thank the reviewer for your constructive comment and positive feedback. The raised typos have been corrected and below we dually respond to your comments.

**Q1**: Proof in the deterministic case will be helpful.

**A1**: Thanks for the helpful suggestion. We will take your advice and reorganize Section 4.2 by (i). first providing the proof in the deterministic case and then (ii). introducing the additional challenges in the stochastic case and how to solve it. Below we sketch the proof in the deterministic case.

**Stage I.** We no longer need surrogate conditioner $	ilde{
u_{t}^i}$ to disentangle the correlation between $m_t$ and $
u_t$ and we only need to handle the mismatch between $m_t$ and $G_t$ in the first-order term $-\eta \langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot m_tangle$. To handle this, we first apply the definition of $m_t = (1- eta_1) (G_t+ eta_1 G_{t-1}+  eta_1^2 G_{t-2}+...) $, and write $$-\eta \langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot m_t angle= -\eta (1- eta_1)( \langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot G_tangle +  eta_1 \langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot G_{t-1}angle+ eta_1^2\langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot G_{t-2}angle+...).$$

We proceed by approximating $G_{t-i}$ by $G_t$ in the above equation, and have 

$$-\eta \langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot m_t angle= -\eta (1- eta_1)( \langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot G_tangle +  eta_1 \langle G_{t-1},  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot G_{t-1}angle+ eta_1^2\langle G_{t-1},  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot G_{t-2}angle+...) +	ext{Error Term}.$$

In the right-hand-side of the above equation, all terms except the "Error Term" (which is in the same order as the second-order term in the descent lemma) are negative. Applying the above equation to the descent lemma and summing it over iterations gives a bound of $\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d  rac{\vert G_{t,l} \vert^2}{\sqrt{
u_{t,l}}}$.

**Stage II.** Based on the observation that $(1- eta_2)G_{t,l}^2= 
u_{t,l}- eta_2 
u_{t-1,l}$, we have $ rac{\vert G_{t,l} \vert^2}{\sqrt{
u_{t,l}}} = \Omega (\sqrt{
u_{t,l}}-\sqrt{ eta_2} \sqrt{
u_{t-1,l}})$, which can transfer the bound of $\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d  rac{\vert G_{t,l} \vert^2}{\sqrt{
u_{t,l}}}$ into a bound of $\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d \sqrt{
u_{t,l}}$. Due to $$ (\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d  rac{\vert G_{t,l} \vert^2}{\sqrt{
u_{t,l}}})(\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d \sqrt{
u_{t,l}})\ge (\sum_{t=1}^T \sum_{l=1}^d  \vert G_{t,l} \vert )^2,$$ applying bounds of $\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d  rac{\vert G_{t,l} \vert^2}{\sqrt{
u_{t,l}}}$ and $\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d \sqrt{
u_{t,l}}$ provides the estimation of gradient norm and concludes the proof.

**Remark**: Compared to the stochastic case, Stage I is much more simplified and no longer needs to disentangle the randomness in $m_t$ and $
u_t$, and Stage II is also much more simplified when transforming the bound of $\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d  rac{\vert G_{t,l} \vert^2}{\sqrt{
u_{t,l}}}$ into a bound of $\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{l=1}^d \sqrt{
u_{t,l}}$.

**Q2**: Can the assumption on noise be weakened to more general conditions mentioned in [1]?

**A2**: Thanks for asking. A brief answer is that the proof can be extended to the more general noise assumption "$E \Vert O_f(w,z) -
abla f(w) \Vert^2 \le \sigma_1^2 \Vert 
abla f(w) \Vert^2+\sigma_0^2$" (called "affine noise variance assumption" in [Faw et al., 2022]), but we are not sure if it can be further generalized under the noise assumption "$E \Vert O_f(w,z) -
abla f(w) \Vert^2 \le \sigma_2^2 (f(w)-f^*)+\sigma_1^2 \Vert 
abla f(w) \Vert^2+\sigma_0^2$".

Below we sketch how to extend the proof under the affine noise variance assumption (**we will provide detailed proof in the appendix of the revised paper**). The core difference of the proofs under different noise assumptions is the way to estimate the first-order term in Stage I. We leverage an auxiliary function $\xi_t=\sum_{l=1}^d rac{\vert G_{t,l} \vert^2 }{\sqrt{	ilde{
u_{t,l}}}}$ to handle the affine variance noise, where $	ilde{
u_t}$ is defined as $	ilde{
u_t}= eta_2
u_{t-1}+(1- eta_2) \sigma_0^2$.

For simplicity, we focus on the case where $ eta_1=0$, i.e., no momentum.  To disentangle the stochasticity between $g_t$ and $
u_t$, we use $	ilde{
u_t}= eta_2
u_{t-1}+(1- eta_2) \sigma_0^2$ to approximate $
u_t$ and thus the first-order term can be written as $$E [\langle G_t,-\eta  rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}}\odot g_t angle]=-\eta E [\langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{	ilde{
u}_t}}\odot G_t angle]+E [\langle G_t,\eta ( rac{1}{\sqrt{	ilde{
u}_t}}- rac{1}{\sqrt{
u_t}})\odot g_t angle].$$ The first term on the right-hand side is the main term (which is negative) and we need to tackle the first term. By leveraging a similar routine as the proof of Lemma 1, we can arrive at the following estimation of the approximation error.

$$	extbf{Approximation Error} \le   rac{1}{4}\eta E [\langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{	ilde{
u_t}}}\odot G_t angle]+O(	ext{Second Order Term})+O(\sum_{l=1}^dE(1- eta_2) rac{ g_{t,l} ^2 G_{t,l}^2}{(\sqrt{
u_{t,l}}+\sqrt{	ilde{
u_{t,l}}})^2\sqrt{	ilde{
u_{t,l}}}} ).$$
In the right-hand-side of the above equation, the last term takes close resemblance to $ rac{\xi_{t-1}}{\sqrt{ eta_2}}-\xi_t$ (after expansion), and indeed can be directly bounded by $O( rac{\xi_{t-1}}{\sqrt{ eta_2}}-\xi_t)$ plus some error term by direct calculation (recall that $\xi_t=\sum_{l=1}^d rac{\Vert G_{t,l} \Vert^2 }{\sqrt{	ilde{
u_{t,l}}}}$). The sum of $O( rac{\xi_{t-1}}{\sqrt{ eta_2}}-\xi_t)$ across t gives $( rac{1}{\sqrt{ eta_2}}-1)O(\sum_{t=1}^T \xi_t)$, which is small than the sum of the main term in the first-order term $-\eta E [\langle G_t,  rac{1}{\sqrt{	ilde{
u}_t}}\odot G_t angle]$ when $ eta_2$ is close to $1$, and thus we succeed to control the approximation error. The rest of the proof should be the same as under the original noise assumption.

